{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["R14", "T4", "T2"],
      "pins": ["T4.1", "T2.3", "R14.1"],
      "nets": ["NET0004", "+3V3"],
      "observed": "On the Main Pump driver, the gate of the low-side N-MOSFET T4 (IRLR2905) is pulled up to +3V3 through R14 (10 kΩ), while the collector of the NPN level-shifter T2 (BT3904) drives the same gate node. The functionally identical ATO-valve driver uses R54 (10 kΩ) as a gate PULL-DOWN to GND.",
      "expected": "A low-side N-MOSFET pre-driven by an NPN level shifter must have its gate pulled down to GND so the switch is OFF when the MCU pin is low or floating (as the ATO-valve driver does with R54, and as the spec's 'low-side switch ... pre-driven via BT3904 NPN level shifter' implies). R14 should terminate at GND, not +3V3.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "MAIN PUMP & VALVE DRIVERS / Main Pump: R14 10k drawn with its top at the +3V3 arrow and bottom at the T4 gate / T2 collector node (NET0004); T2 BT3904 emitter to GND; T4 IRLR2905"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve: R54 10k drawn from the T3 gate node (NET0024) down to GND (T3 source) — opposite convention"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T2 — IRLR2905 ... drives PUMP_MAIN net to the 24 V main pump load; gate driven through R1 (100 Ω) from the ESP32 pin, pre-driven via T1 (BT3904 NPN level shifter)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The main circulation pump is energised whenever GPIO IO10 is low or floating, i.e. it runs continuously at power-on until firmware actively drives the pin high, and the drive polarity is inverted relative to the ATO valve. A pump that starts by itself at boot (and before firmware configures the GPIO) is a dry-run / control failure.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["T3"],
      "pins": ["T3.1", "T3.2", "T3.3"],
      "nets": ["ATO_VALVE", "NET0008", "+24V"],
      "observed": "The ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch T3 is populated with an AO3400A (30 V N-MOSFET); the AON6407 (60 V) part that the spec assigns to the valve is instead used as the input reverse-polarity switch T1.",
      "expected": "Per the spec the ATO-valve switch is the 60 V AON6407 ('T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch'), chosen for headroom on the 24 V inductive solenoid load.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve: T3 AO3400A on the ATO_VALVE/NET0008 drain node, load V2 DIGITEN-24V, flyback D6 SS34 to +24V"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 30 V-rated MOSFET on a 24 V rail with an inductive solenoid has very little voltage headroom against switching transients/ringing beyond the SS34 clamp, risking avalanche breakdown and premature failure of the valve driver.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design in the actuator drivers: the main-pump MOSFET gate is pulled up to +3V3 (R14) instead of down to GND, so the circulation pump is ON by default / active-low, and the ATO-valve switch uses a 30 V AO3400A where the spec calls for the 60 V AON6407."
}

